WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:05.160
- Today, I'm pleased to
announce nominations and staff

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.280
for critical foreign policy and
national security positions.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.500
Key nominations and appointments

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.290
for the critical economic positions.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:15.170
I'm really pleased to
add members to my team

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.540
that'll get the job done.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.090
- It's a rule of thumb in Washington

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.940
that personnel is policy.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.900
That is to say, a new president's choices

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.230
for top jobs in his administration
tell you an awful lot

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.370
about where that
administration will be headed.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.680
We've now seen President-elect
Joe Biden make more

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.640
than two dozen choices for top jobs

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.190
in the administration that
will take office in January,

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.050
and that's enough to draw some conclusions

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.160
about the kind of team he's put together.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.330
The first thing to say, perhaps,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.940
is that Mr. Biden clearly
chose not to let the dispute

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.130
about the outcome of the 2020 election,

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.280
those lawsuits President Trump
and his campaign have filed

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.040
to try to overturn the results,

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:49.930
from getting in the way

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:52.780
of moving forward on the personnel front.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.220
You know, under similar
circumstances in 2000,

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.250
when the outcome of the election
again was under dispute,

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.440
the new president, then George W. Bush,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.580
chose not to make choices
for top jobs until December,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.920
and some people think that
put his new administration

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.310
behind the curve a
little bit at the outset.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.170
That's clearly a risk Mr.
Biden chose not to take.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.610
Beyond that, there are several
notable characteristics

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.970
that mark the emerging Biden team.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.320
First, it's center left, not far left.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.230
Joe Biden campaigned and
won as a centrist Democrat,

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.430
and against the more progressive
wing of his own party,

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.780
and his team reflects that.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.570
There's no Senator Bernie Sanders.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.210
There's no Senator Elizabeth Warren.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.610
They're the favorites of
Democratic progressives.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.130
Instead, Mr. Biden has tended more

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.840
toward fellow veterans of
the Obama administration,

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.800
people with establishment credentials,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.210
and people who have worked
with Mr. Biden for years,

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.370
including his Chief of Staff, Ron Klain,

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.610
his National Security
Advisor Jake Sullivan,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.750
his White House Counselor Steve Ricchetti,

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.930
his Secretary of State Tony Blinken,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.760
and the latest addition to that list,

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.370
and the surprising one, Susan Rice,

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.610
who's moved from being a
national security expert

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.830
to Head of the Domestic Policy Council.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.460
Second, this is not
exactly a team of rivals.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.650
You know when Barack Obama
won the presidency in 2008,

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.430
he famously picked his top
Democratic primary opponent

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.450
that year, Hillary Clinton,
to be his Secretary of State,

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.240
arguably the most important
person in his Cabinet.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.950
- I have known Hillary Clinton
as a friend, a colleague,

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:19.680
a source of counsel, and
a tough campaign opponent.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.400
She possesses an
extraordinary intelligence,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.440
and a remarkable work ethic.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.170
I am proud that she will be
our next Secretary of State.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.410
- Well, that hasn't been
so much the pattern here.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.330
Joe Biden obviously picked Kamala Harris,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.840
a primary season opponent,
to be his running mate,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.450
and he's just chosen Pete Buttigieg,

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.390
the former mayor of South Bend, Indiana,

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.070
and another opponent in the primaries

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:42.050
to be Secretary of Transportation.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.890
But most of the top jobs have instead gone

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.370
to people who Joe Biden has known

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.920
for years in other capacities,
such as General Lloyd Austin,

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.720
who was the leader of troops in Iraq

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.670
when Joe Biden was vice-president,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.140
they got to know each other then,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.750
he's picked to be now
Secretary of Defense.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.510
Third, this team is diverse.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.200
Mr. Biden said during the campaign,

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.890
and repeatedly since the election,

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.650
that he wants to pick a team
that looks like America,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.800
and his choices clearly
reflect a desire to do that.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.930
- I said at the outset,
I wanted to represent,

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:14.440
this campaign to represent
and look like America.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.710
We've done that.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.300
Now that's what I want the administration

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.280
to look like and act like.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.770
For all those of you.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.320
- Some firsts have been set here.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.780
The White House communications team

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.520
will be made up of all women.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.100
The Treasury Secretary will be a woman.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.180
Her deputy will be a black man.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.120
The former Head of the
Congressional Black Caucus

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.000
will be a top White House advisor.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.110
The Director of National
Intelligence will be a woman

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.370
for the first time.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.320
Some on the left will say

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.560
there should have been more diversity,

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.210
but some critics will say
there has been some diversity

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.370
for diversity's sake, and
they'll probably point

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.200
at General Austin at the Pentagon,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.730
or California Attorney
General Xavier Becerra

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.640
at the Department of
Health and Human Services,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.560
despite the fact he doesn't have a health

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.310
or a medical background.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.680
Fourth, most of these picks
will not be controversial,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.970
but a few may be.

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:57.450
It's rare for a new president not

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.710
to get the Senate to go along

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.710
with his top choices to fill
out his new administration,

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.180
but it's not unheard of.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.290
The most notable exception

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.000
in recent times probably came in 1988,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.430
when the new president, George H. W. Bush,

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.440
picked a former Senator, John Tower,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.030
to be a Secretary of Defense,

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.650
and the Senate rejected that pick.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.630
- I will be recorded as
the first Cabinet nominee

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.140
in the history of the
Republic to be rejected

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.920
in the first 90 days of a presidency,

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.210
and perhaps be harshly judged.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.600
- If Republicans control
the Senate after a couple

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.230
of runoff races in Georgia in January,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.150
they'll be in a position

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.000
to block President-elect Biden's picks

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.160
if they choose to do so,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.290
and they may make one

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.210
or two choices to make that point clear.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.290
One of the people who's perhaps vulnerable

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.380
in that regard is Neera Tanden,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.530
picked to head the Office
of Management and Budget,

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.560
and who's riled up Republican senators

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.520
with some of her partisan
comments over the years.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:51.830
As he finishes out his team,

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:52.820
it'll be interesting to see

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.340
whether Mr. Biden goes for
a couple of Republicans

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.910
for offices, just to make real his pledge

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.150
that he wants to work across
the aisle with the other party.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.870
Bottom line is that this
team, as is often the case,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.910
reflects the president that has chosen it.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:06.960
That is to say,

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.203
it's long on Washington
experience, and center left.

